				<table cellpadding=5 cellspacing=0 border=0>
					<tr>
						<td style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold" colspan="2">
							Report: Giuliani Employs Barred Priest
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td style="font-size: 11pt;"> 
							 
							<br>
							<br>
							05:53 AM EST February 01, 2003 
							<br>
							The Associated Press<br><br><br>NEW YORK<p class="nitfp">A priest who was barred from the ministry after he was accused of sexual abuse has been working for former Mayor Rudolph Giuliani's consulting business, The New York Times reported Saturday.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Monsignor Alan Placa, an old friend of Giuliani's, has been working for Giuliani Partners about three days a week since August.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"He's a very prominent guy and an incredibly learned man and able man," Giuliani spokeswoman Sunny Mindel told The Times.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Last April, Bishop William Murphy of the Diocese of Rockville Centre removed Monsignor Placa, 58, from a panel investigating clerical sexual abuse after several families claimed Placa had used his role as a spiritual adviser to get information from victims that would bolster the diocese's legal position. Placa has denied the charge.</p>
<p class="nitfp">In June, Murphy placed Placa on leave after allegations surfaced that he molested a teenage seminarian about 25 years ago. An investigation by the Nassau County district attorney has not been completed.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Giuliani has been a steadfast supporter of Placa, who received special permission to preside over the funeral of the former mayor's mother in September.</p>
<p class="nitfp">A call to the diocese early Saturday went unanswered. The Times said Placa could not immediately be reached for comment. He has previously denied the allegations of sexual abuse.</p>

